# dfiles - File Explorer

"dfiles" or "File Explorer" is a small native file manager that also provides an XDG Desktop Portal
file chooser. One executable owns both entry points: the normal file manager
and the D-Bus-activated portal backend. They share the same CreamUI browser.

File Explorer intentionally does not own `org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop`; the
standard `xdg-desktop-portal` broker owns that API. It also contains no desktop
or distro policy. A distro chooses File Explorer by installing its `.portal` manifest
and its own `*-portals.conf` preference file.

## Run locally

```sh
cargo run --bin dfiles

# Run the backend directly for D-Bus development.
cargo run --bin dfiles -- --portal
```

The normal portal deployment is D-Bus activation. A distribution installs
`data/dfiles.portal` and
`data/org.freedesktop.impl.portal.desktop.dfiles.service`, then selects the
`dfiles` backend from that distribution's portal policy.

`dfiles --portal` is deliberately a launch mode, not a second binary. It keeps
installation, updates, and the D-Bus service aligned with the file manager.

## Browser MVP

The app and portal share a system-themed CreamUI layout with a soft sidebar,
tabs, an editable address bar, back/forward/up navigation, refresh, and a
search field that filters the current view by name. Each tab keeps its own
history, search, scroll position, selection, and list/grid preference. Tabs
page when the window is too narrow to show them all.

- Home shows pinned folders, local mounted drives with free space, and files
  recently opened through File Explorer. Pin the current or selected folder with
  **Pin folder**; remove it with **Unpin** or its sidebar ×.
- **Your files** uses `user-dirs.dirs` from `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` (with conventional
  folder names as a fallback), and only includes folders that exist.
- Drives include the root filesystem and local devices mounted under `/media`,
  `/run/media`, or `/mnt`. Refresh updates the mounted drives and free space.
- Click to select; double-click or press Enter on a focused item to open it.
  The address bar accepts absolute/relative paths, `~`, and local `file://` URIs.
- The portal supports opening a file, choosing a folder, and choosing a save
  destination with an editable name and explicit replacement confirmation.
  It honors the requesting app's initial folder, accept label, file filters,
  selected filter, and modal hint. Glob and MIME filters are applied to the
  browser and the selected filter is sent back in the portal response.
  Cancel/closing returns no path.

Window construction requests the same compositor **Hybrid** integration as
Coconut Settings, including the Blair integration feature. The tab strip
reserves compositor control space; client controls appear only when CreamUI
negotiates client decorations. Empty header areas remain draggable.

Pinned and recently opened paths persist as file URIs in
`$XDG_STATE_HOME/dfiles/` (default `~/.local/state/dfiles/`). Hidden files are
omitted. Search is local to the current view, not recursive. Cloud drives,
tags, mounting/ejecting, file operations, thumbnails, xdg-foreign parenting,
and portal multiple selection/choices are outside this MVP. Unsupported portal
options fail explicitly instead of silently changing the request.

## Structure

`src/app.rs` creates the shared hybrid window; `src/window.rs` describes the
launch mode; `src/context.rs` provides `use_is_portal()` to the interface.
`src/portal/` owns D-Bus, option parsing, filter matching, and portal types;
`src/browser/`, `src/navigation.rs`, `src/components/`, and `src/views/` own
the shared browser state and UI. A router is unnecessary: each file tab keeps
its own navigation history and active location.

## Validation and native previews

```sh
cargo test --all-targets
cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
DFILES_SNAPSHOT_DIR=/tmp/dfiles-preview cargo test native_layout_and_portal_previews
```

The preview test renders the actual CreamUI tree without a display server,
using temporary filesystem fixtures. It exports Home, list, grid, open/save
portal, compact, and dark appearances; it does not install the portal or alter
the user's folders or pinned state.
